[x] a free supscription user can add music to playlist but can not edit it even if it his own playlist. This is a limitation of the free subscription plan. To edit playlists, users need to upgrade to a premium subscription. (Covered by the existing public-playlist premium gate — see docs/SUBSCRIPTION_BONUS.md.)
[x] if a user the user is upgraded to primium subscription then downgraded either by clicking the downgrade button or by canceling the subscription, the user will lose the ability to edit their playlists.  However, they will still be able to view and play their playlists, but any changes made while on a premium subscription will remain intact. it is not woking right now for the the playlists that the user was invited to (Premium gate broadened to cover private playlists too, not just public — editing any playlist, owned or invited, now requires both access and Premium. backend/playlists/permissions.py, mobile playlist_detail_screen.dart/subscription_screen.dart/premium_checkout_screen.dart.)
[x] in the subscription management section, users can see their current subscription status and the benefits associated with each plan. It is important to clearly communicate the limitations of the free subscription to avoid confusion and ensure users understand what features are available to them. (subscription_screen.dart's perk list now switches to "FREE PLAN LIMITS" wording with explicit Premium-vs-Free numbers when on Free.)
[x] in the subscription management section, users must be able to see how much time is left on their current subscription and when it will expire. This information helps users make informed decisions about whether to renew or upgrade their subscription. (Added User.subscription_expires_at, set to +30 days on upgrade/cleared on downgrade in SubscriptionSwitchView; shown as "Renews on <date> · N days left" on the tier card.)


[x] when edditing playlist , user must be able to see that they are currently on a free subscription and that editing is not available. A prompt or message should inform them that they need to upgrade to a premium subscription to access editing features. even if the user needs to request invitation to edit the playlist there must be to two messages so that the user is aware of both the limitation of their current subscription and the option to request an invitation to edit the playlist. This ensures clarity and transparency in the user experience. (playlist_detail_screen.dart now shows the Premium banner and the invited-only/request-access banner independently — both appear together when both conditions are unmet.)



[x] if a user is invited to edit a playlist they must receive a notification informing them of the invitation. (Realtime toast already fired on invite via notify_user(kind="playlist_invite") in backend/playlists/views_collaborators.py. Added it to the persistent in-app Notifications list too: new GET /playlists/collaborators/mine/ endpoint, surfaced in notifications_screen.dart as a dismissible "invited to collaborate" card for invites from the last 7 days.)

[] in the events section, free subscription users should be limited to 10 suggestions and 20 votes per day, not per events, and it must be clearly communicated to users that these limits apply to their overall activity across all events. This ensures that users understand the scope of their limitations and can plan their participation accordingly. (event_detail_screen.dart now shows a "10 suggestions / 20 votes per day" message when on Free, and the backend enforces the limit across all events, not per event.)


[x] add joined button to playlist (self-serve join for public playlists — PlaylistMembership/is_member, PlaylistJoinView, "Join playlist"/"Joined" button on playlist detail screen)

[x] fix the screen of notification, it must display all the notification and must save the notification read/unread status. (Currently, the notification screen is not displaying all notifications and does not save the read/unread status.) (Added a persisted `Notification` model — every existing `notify_user()` call site now writes a row alongside its realtime websocket toast, no call sites changed. New GET /user/notifications/, POST .../<id>/read/, POST .../read-all/. notifications_screen.dart now has an "All notifications" history section below the actionable cards, with unread/read styling and mark-on-tap; home_screen.dart's bell badge now reflects the real unread count instead of an ad-hoc sum.)

[x] if an invited user is granted the ability to invite and remove people , the user must be able to see the list of invited users and their status (accepted, pending, or declined). This allows the user to manage their playlist effectively and ensure that only authorized individuals have access to edit the playlist. (The list already existed in PlaylistCollaboratorsScreen — ACCESS REQUESTS section = pending, COLLABORATORS section = accepted; declined ones aren't kept around. The real gap was that a manager-collaborator (can_manage_collaborators) couldn't see or decide access requests — those endpoints were owner-only. Extended PlaylistAccessRequestListCreateView.get/PlaylistAccessRequestDecideView.post in backend/playlists/views_access_requests.py to use can_user_manage_collaborators like invite/remove already did; added tests. Editing a collaborator's own permissions stays owner-only.)
[x] also fix the screen of the user who is granted this privilege by the owner, so that they can also invite people. (playlist_detail_screen.dart's showInvitationControls was isOwner-only, hiding the Invite/Manage entry point from a collaborator with can_manage_collaborators. Now checks that flag too. PlaylistCollaboratorsScreen also disables — rather than hides — the per-collaborator permission switches for a non-owner manager, since changing permissions stays owner-only.)
